E2AP and E2SM-KPM message definitions
    Generated by asn1c from the O-RAN ASN.1 definitions, and taken from the
    O-RAN Software Community. E2AP v3.01 and E2SM-KPM v3.00.

    One generation covering both, rather than a separate library for each.
    The two are otherwise built from different asn1c runs whose runtime files
    disagree, and a binary cannot hold both.

    Renamed here, contents unchanged: GlobalGNB-ID and GlobalNgENB-ID carry
    the E2SM-COMMON-IEs prefix their siblings already use. Go rejects a
    package holding two filenames that differ only in case, which these did
    against the E2AP types of nearly the same name.

wrapper.c, wrapper.h
    Derived from O-RAN Software Community ric-app/kpimon-go, Apache 2.0.

    Changes made here, all Apache 2.0:

    Added, because the upstream wrapper has no equivalent:
      - e2ap_decode_indication, which takes the service model payloads out of
        the E2AP message a RAN reports with.
      - e2sm_decode_ran_function_description and its accessors, so an xApp can
        read what a RAN advertises rather than assume it.
      - e2sm_decode_ric_action_definition, e2sm_decode_ric_event_trigger_
        definition and their accessors, so an encoded subscription can be read
        back and checked.
      - accessors for the measurement names, values and period an indication
        carries.

    Fixed, because a library must not do these things:
      - Encoders wrote progress and failure text to stderr, including on
        success. Removed from the functions this package calls.
      - The action definition encoders returned -1 from a size_t, which reaches
        the caller as a very large positive number. They now return ssize_t,
        matching the event trigger encoder in the same file.
      - The event trigger encoder never assigned the reporting period it is
        given, leaving a mandatory field at zero so encoding always failed its
        constraint. It now uses the period passed to it. Upstream does not call
        this function; its own xApp sends a hard-coded byte sequence instead.
      - The event trigger was encoded with unaligned PER while everything else
        here, encoders and decoders alike, uses aligned PER. A RAN read a
        reporting period of 1 ms where 1000 was asked for. The unaligned call
        also reports its length in bits rather than bytes.

    Carried across to the newer definitions: a matching condition is now
    reached through a choice member rather than directly.

    The message construction is otherwise unchanged.
